371:      <section class="chapter-section idea-cycle" id="idea-stop">
373:        <h2 class="section-title">Tactic #1: Stop Coding</h2>
383:          <h3>The ninety-percent trap</h3>
391:          <h3>Do not remove engineering; move it around the code</h3>
424:          <h3>The tactic is said twice because the easy interpretation is wrong</h3>
435:          <h3>Stop coding ≠ stop engineering</h3>
436:          <aside class="callout callout--term">
451:          <h3>Zero application-code keystrokes in TAC-1</h3>
457:          <aside class="callout callout--why">
465:          <h3>Use the next imperfection as the exercise</h3>
478:          <h3>Two opposite misunderstandings</h3>
480:            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>“No more code”</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p><strong>Why it fails:</strong> code remains a primary asset that produces features and products. TAC expects you to generate more of it.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> say “stop hand-typing” every time you teach the tactic.</p></div></article>
481:            <article class="comparison__card"><div class="comparison__head"><h3>“No need to understand code”</h3></div><div class="comparison__body"><p><strong>Why it fails:</strong> faster generation magnifies the cost of weak review and weak system judgment.</p><p><strong>Fix:</strong> move reading, execution, tests, and diff review into explicit pass criteria.</p></div></article>
488:          <h3>The tactic has a public companion</h3>
489:          <aside class="current-practice">
506:          <section class="retrieval-quiz" aria-label="Retrieval questions for Stop Coding">
507:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">1. What exactly stops when you follow Tactic #1?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Your hand-typing of application code. Code generation and code understanding continue.</p></details></div>
508:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">2. Where do the freed engineering cycles go?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>Planning, reviewing, and creating closed-loop structures.</p></details></div>
509:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">3. Why is fixing the last two lines by hand a course failure even when it is faster?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>It completes the artifact while hiding the loop’s weakness. You lose the chance to improve agent instructions, feedback, and verification for repeatable future work.</p></details></div>
510:            <div class="retrieval-quiz__item"><p class="retrieval-quiz__q">4. What must increase when agents generate more code?</p><details class="retrieval-quiz__a"><summary>Reveal answer</summary><p>The quality and systematization of planning, review, validation, and governance.</p></details></div>
